WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.564
The year is 1979.

00:00:02.066 --> 00:00:04.273
You work in Denis Baylor's laboratory at
Stanford.

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:08.401
A suction electrode holds one rod outer segment
from a toad's retina.

00:00:09.192 --> 00:00:12.959
You give the rod very dim flashes of light, and
most flashes do nothing.

00:00:13.561 --> 00:00:17.225
The flashes that do give a response all give
about the same response.

00:00:17.789 --> 00:00:22.156
It is 1 pA, which is 5% of the current that the
rod carries in the dark.

00:00:22.752 --> 00:00:24.780
One photon changes one molecule.

00:00:25.384 --> 00:00:28.233
Between that molecule and your recording are
four parts.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.800
You have met each of them in another form:

00:00:31.253 --> 00:00:33.049
- a receptor (rhodopsin),

00:00:33.564 --> 00:00:35.600
- a G protein (transducin),

00:00:36.189 --> 00:00:40.685
- an enzyme that destroys a cyclic nucleotide (a
phosphodiesterase),

00:00:41.235 --> 00:00:43.630
- ion channels that cGMP holds open.

00:00:44.384 --> 00:00:45.924
You decide how they are arranged.

00:00:46.431 --> 00:00:47.710
Answer three questions.

00:00:48.522 --> 00:00:51.749
1. Which steps multiply the number of molecules?

00:00:52.272 --> 00:00:54.948
How would you tell them apart from steps that
cannot multiply?

00:00:55.778 --> 00:00:59.691
2. Which step limits the size of the response,
and why?

00:01:00.458 --> 00:01:06.799
3. What would the cell change to make the
response last 10 times longer, without making it
10 times larger?

00:01:07.128 --> 00:01:12.732
Here is how the rod works, from the molecule
that absorbs the photon to the change in your
recording.

00:01:13.508 --> 00:01:18.555
In the dark, the rod has a steady leak of
current, and this is what the electrode
measures.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:25.211
Ion channels in the membrane of the outer
segment are held open by a small molecule, cGMP.

00:01:25.917 --> 00:01:28.196
Ions flow through these channels all the time.

00:01:28.832 --> 00:01:31.279
This is the dark current of about 20 pA.

00:01:31.797 --> 00:01:37.804
A photon is absorbed by one molecule of
rhodopsin, the light-sensitive receptor protein
of the rod.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:41.399
The photon changes rhodopsin into its active
shape.

00:01:42.031 --> 00:01:47.250
This one active rhodopsin switches on many
molecules of a G protein, transducin.

00:01:47.828 --> 00:01:50.889
Transducin can act only after it binds GTP.

00:01:51.506 --> 00:01:58.274
Each active transducin switches on an enzyme, a
phosphodiesterase, that destroys cGMP.

00:01:58.974 --> 00:02:04.545
The cGMP level falls, so some of the channels
close, and for a moment the current is smaller.

00:02:05.204 --> 00:02:07.423
Your electrode recorded this brief drop.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.903
One rhodopsin molecule could never carry 1 pA of
current by itself, and it does not need to.

00:02:14.759 --> 00:02:17.747
Each step passes its work to more copies of the
next step.

00:02:18.699 --> 00:02:23.134
The cell keeps a current running in the dark,
and the photon only has to reduce it.
